        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "B–6 For each point P in B, let  $ S_P $ be the set of points with all coordinates equal to  $ \\pm 1 $ which differ from P in exactly one coordinate. Since there are more than  $ 2^{n+1}/n $ points in B, and each  $ S_P $ has n elements, the cardinalities of the sets  $ S_P $ add up to more than  $ 2^{n+1} $, which is to say, more than twice the total number of points. By the pigeonhole principle, there must be a point in three of the sets, say  $ S_P, S_Q, S_R $. But then any two of P, Q, R differ in exactly two coordinates, so PQR is an equilateral triangle, as desired.",
